duation when they  are attacked by a killer in a skull mask  the pool is a by the numbers slasher no  one will be surprised when the  characters split away from the group  just so they can die quicker or the  killer's deeply misogynistic motivation  you know the pool has nothing original  to say when it includes the actual line  i know what you did last summer  m night shyamalan's 2000 feature  unbreakable has developed a massive cult  following for its original take on the  superhero formula 16 years after its  premiere fans were excited when the  ending to shyamalan's feature film split  revealed that the movie was a secret  sequel shyamalan completed his trilogy  with 2019's glass bringing together each  film's protagonist for the first time  in front of the world  in the 19 years between unbreakable and  glass superhero narratives have gone  from nerdy niche to blockbuster fodder  shyamalan subverts the genre in glass  making a superhero film that feels  deliberately underwhelming even  alienating to its audience ultimately  whatever statement the film was trying  to make about superheroes becomes  pointless as david ehrlich said in his  film review for indiewire the trouble  with glass is that its mildly intriguing  meta textual narrative is so much richer  and more compelling than the asinine  story that shyamalan tells on its  surface although many including  shyamalan were impressed with mcavoy's  performance that alone couldn't rescue  the film  my son gives james mcavoy one of the  most challenging roles of his career as  a father investigating the disappearance  of his young son he was also the only  actor on the set of the 2021 mystery  thriller not given a script left up to  largely improvise his performance which  created a unique experience where the  audience uncovers clues and deciphers  the mystery at the same time the actor  does director co-writer christian  carrillo adapted my son from his 2017  french film of the same name which would  give one hope that he had ironed out the  kinks of such an unusual production  mcavoy gives a naturalistic performance  capturing a father's confusion anxiety  and rage but the film doesn't have much  to offer audiences aside from the  central gimmick my son could have been  groundbreaking but it's only  conventional  it's the early 1980s and mcavoy's  professor x dresses like he's on miami  vice as he instructs a new class of  super students meanwhile the ancient  mutant and sabonur played by oscar isaac  under pounds of blue makeup seeks to  rule the world i  have returned  x-men apocalypse has its charms such as  when evan peters quicksilver returns for  a rescue scene set to the music by the  band eurythmics but brian singer's final  film in the franchise he launched in  2000 is uneven and overstuffed even die  hard x-men fans will groan at the film's  many detours including the obligatory  wolverine cameo somehow holocaust  survivor magneto destroying auschwitz  out of grief and rage is in the same  movie with cody smith mcphee's  nightcrawler wearing michael jackson's  jacket from thriller they may save the  world but for the x-men this film is a  sign of the end times  welcome to the punch is a standard  shoot-em-up starring mcavoy as max  lewinsky a cop still dealing with the  effects of being shot by career criminal  jacob sternwood played by mark strong  several shootouts follow all leading to  a final confrontation at the container  yard known as the punch mcevoy and  strong are excellent foils to one  another but their sparks aren't enough  to light up this derivative cops and  robbers movie it's a shame because one  wonders what the two leads could have  done with a more interesting script  welcome to the punch is a decent time  waster for action junkies but not much  else  also released under the title rory  o'shea was here the 2004 irish dramedy  inside i'm dancing stars mcavoy as the  titular rory o'shea an irrepressible  rebel who lives with duchenne muscular  dystrophy he befriends the introverted  michael played by stephen robertson who  has cerebral palsy but this being a kind  of inspirational film tragedy inevitably  strikes 18 years after its release  inside i'm dancing deserves scrutiny for  casting two able-bodied actors as the  disabled wheelchair using rory and  michael as andrew paul rang a disabled  writer said of the issue in forbes what  to some viewers seemed to be deeply  empathetic and almost athletic feats of  acting to others in particular many  actual disabled people seem more like  showboating and mockery the film  attempts to address ableism in ireland  including the difficulty of finding  affordable accessible housing but inside  i'm dancing undermines its message with  problematic casting and a sappy  stereotypical story  christina ricci plays the titular  penelope a young woman born with a pig  snout as a result of a witch's curse  with her snout hidden under a colorful  scarf she falls for mcavoy's johnny  marsh a red-blooded gambler while her  wealthy parents arrange for a rich  suitor to supposedly break the curse god  you licked me edward what don't lick max  penelope is an eccentric fairy tale that  helped cement mcevoy's status as a  romantic lead viewers watching today  will be star struck by the supporting  cast which includes catherine o'hara  richard e grant reese witherspoon and  peter dinklage the actors gamely play  along with the film's quirky plot and  uplifting final message about  self-confidence but there may be a limit  to how many pig jokes they can make  robert redford's 2010 period drama the  conspirator explores a little-known  chapter of american history the trial of  mary sarat who is played by robin wright  mary's son john was one of the  conspirators behind president abraham  lincoln's assassination and because mary  owned the boarding house where john and  his accomplices gathered she was also  charged as a conspirator you were one of  lincoln's pallbearers too how can you  represent her she's entitled to a  defense freddie  so i shall defend her  in the conspirator mcavoy's frederick  aiken is a lawyer who doesn't want to  defend surat in court she is a proud  southerner and he was injured fighting  for the union in the civil war but after  meeting mary and her daughter anna  played by evan rachel wood he becomes  convinced of her innocence it isn't  often that a movie based on true events  has a red carpet screening at the scene  of the crime but the conspirator was  shown at ford's theater in washington dc  the site of lincoln's assassination a  bold choice but one that reflects the  overall staginess of a well-acted but  sometimes boring film a perfect movie  for high school history teachers to show  their students and sometimes that's  enough  becoming jane joyfully mixes fact and  fiction to tell the story of novelist  jane austen played by anne hathaway and  her brief love affair with mcavoy's tom  lefroy an irish lawyer set before she  wrote her groundbreaking novels austin  is living in the country her life is  upended by the arrival of lefroy and in  true romantic novel fashion sparks fly  the chemistry between hathaway and  mcavoy carries this light but  bittersweet film particularly in a dance  sequence full of smoldering romantic  tension perfect scenery and beautifully  detailed costumes add to the film's  elegance watching it is like having tea  on a lovely sunday morning dedicated  austin fans may object to becoming jane  favoring a fictionalized love story over  her literary achievements but the film  makes an argument for laphroaig  inspiring mr darcy the romantic hero of  austin's pride and prejudice  they don't make rom-coms like 2004's  wimbledon anymore the movie stars paul  bettany is peter an aging british tennis  pro who intends to retire after the  upcoming wimbledon tournament an  unexpected romance with vivacious rising  star lizzie played by kirsten dunst  reignites his love for the sport however  her father played by sam neil sees him  as a distraction that could ruin her  career in one of his early supporting  roles james mcavoy delivers comic relief  as carl peter's cyclist brother who bets  against his games hold on hold on isn't  he playing your brother  you should be ashamed of yourself yeah  but curiously i'm not  wimbledon is as pleasant and uneventful  as a warm bath and betany and dunst are  charming as two pretty blonde people who  fall in love it doesn't break the mold  but in an era where mainstream  theatrically released rom-coms are an  endangered species wimbledon becomes  something special  it was the horror blockbuster of 2017  and currently ranks as the highest  grossing movie based on a stephen king  novel telling the story of derry maine  the home of the otherworldly clown  pennywise and the losers club who try to  destroy it director andy muscati chose  to adapt the book in two parts it  chapter 2 picks up with the adult  characters 27 years after the first film  what  push i'm pulling [ __ ] oh  thanks  james mcavoy inherited the role of bill  the leader of the losers club and now a  successful horror novelist he reunites  with his friends including jessica  chastain's bev and bill haters richie to  perform the ritual of chud and end  pennywise's threat forever it chapter 2  is dragged down by a bloated 169 minute  run time and a plot overflowing with  aggravating detours while it has a  strong cast and scary surprises it  chapter 2 can't quite recapture its  childhood glory  based on evelyn was 1930 novel vile  bodies stephen fry's 2003 film bright  young things chronicles the lives of the  idol rich as they delve into parties  alcohol and sex adam played by stephen  campbell moore wants to marry social  climber nina portrayed by emily mortimer  unfortunately customs officers  confiscate the novel he has staked their  future on calling it pornography mcavoy  plays the aristocratic and bohemian lord  simon balcam  a small but significant character as he  is the face behind mr chatterbox  london's most notorious scandal column  simon stark quickly falls when he's  caught crashing the wrong party and  facing social ruin commits suicide  though he exits at the halfway mark  mcavoy's presence lingers over the film  as adam only inherits the mr chatterbox  column through simon's death beneath the  glitz and glamour of bright young things  their lurks a terrible human cost  as an actor james mcavoy is not afraid  of taking on unusual or challenging  roles the disappearance of eleanor rigby  is three films in one chronicling the  decaying marriage between mcavoy's conor  and his wife eleanor played by jessica  chastain  the disappearance of eleanor rigby her  and the disappearance of eleanor rigby  him tell the same story from each point  of view a fight between conor and  eleanor in one film could have an  entirely new context in the other  creating a unique viewing experience  director ned benson also pieced him and  her into a single film subtitled them  that tells conor and eleanor stories in  a single linear timeline them is the  most conventional of the three with  jason bailey noting its incompleteness  for flavor wire it's a very good film  but throughout it it's phantom limbs  tingle hinting that it was something  much more special before its  frankenstein job what could have been a  novelty project emerges as a deep drama  about two people who come together come  apart and maybe come together again for  those who can commit to multiple movies  be prepared for a rewarding experience  no one can say that 2013's filth doesn't  live up to its title mcavoy stars is  bruce robertson a corrupt cop who brings  pain and misery wherever he goes when  his usual vices aren't enough bruce  tramples his way through edinburgh his  mental state fracturing into disturbing  hallucinations and things only get worse  from there i used to be a good person  yeah  i heard that  filth is an endurance test sprung from  the mind of ervin welsh the author of  trainspotting the film is manic  self-indulgent raw and savage if the  audience isn't hiding behind their hands  they won't be able to take their eyes  off james mcavoy in a performance  completely stripped of vanity mcavoy  plunges into the darkest parts of  bruce's soul revealing the terrible  losses that sent him into a  self-destructive spiral lest the viewer  think spilth has gone soft it saves the  cruelest joke for the very last scene  mcavoy simon is a fine art auctioneer in  danny boyle's 2013 featured trance who  believes that art is not comparable to  human lives the human wreckage left in  the wake of a botched artist seems to  prove him wrong in this wild  psychological thriller simon somehow  engineers a switch of a priceless goya  painting before it's stolen but after  getting a nasty blow on the head during  the heist he can't remember what he did  with the original oh yes trans is an  amnesia movie from spellbound to memento  amnesia is an advantage to psychological  thrillers especially ones about how the  slipperiness of memory shapes our  identities simon seeks out the  hypnotherapist elizabeth played by  rosario dawson to unblock his memory but  when she learns what he's trying to find  she wants a piece of the action but what  else is simon hiding in the depths of  his mind boyle's film is full of pulpy  nightmarish excess to the point where  reality seems to bend rewriting memories  and personalities at will did you ever  dream about waking up one day and  finding out that you're not an ordinary  joe working in a cubicle that you're the  lost heir to a secret fraternity of rich  beautiful assassins and now you can do  anything you want this geeky power  fantasy comes alive in 2008's wanted  loosely based on the comic series of the  same name by mark miller and jg jones  wanted shifts away from the comics  nihilistic tone and cheap shocks relying  much more on kinetic escapism mcavoy in  a role originally modeled after the  rapper eminem gets the action movie  equivalent of a cinderella makeover he  seamlessly transforms from a sweaty  anxious schlub into a leather jacket  wearing dual wielding elite assassin  wanted also gave mcavoy and future  guardian of the galaxy star chris pratt  their first roles in a comic book movie  making it even more hilarious when  wesley slams a keyboard into his  obnoxious co-workers face  the last station accomplishes a rare  feat it spins a vital engaging film  around the subject of copyright law the  2009 feature is set in 1910 focusing on  the final months of iconic russian  author leo tolstoy played by the late  great christopher plummer tolstoy  preaches ideals such as pacifism and the  abolishment of private property and  considers signing a new will that would  put all of his writings in the public  domain however his wife sophia portrayed  by helen mirren who bore him 13 children  fears that he is robbing his family of  their rightful inheritance despite good  cause for it  i've never stopped loving you of course  but god knows you don't make it easy  woven into this family conflict is  tolstoy's private secretary valentin  played by mcavoy young and idealistic  and prone to sneezing fits valentin  realizes that his literary idol is not a  god but a flawed man who doesn't always  practice what he preaches helen mirren  owns the film with her explosive  performance as sophia a woman who sees  straight through tolstoy's suck-ups and  bristles at her husband's betrayal  2003's bollywood queen was the first  time james mcavoy stepped into the role  of romeo in a modern reimagining of  william shakespeare's romeo and juliet  but not the last thankfully he isn't  playing a cartoon gnome here he's jay a  guitar strumming lad from somerset who  falls in love with gina played by priya  khalidus a british indian girl who loves  bollywood musicals bollywood queen is  bright and splashy fun given its  inspiration it's not a shock when  characters start dancing and bursting  into songs both in hindi and english  cletus and mcavoy are cute as the couple  caught in the clash between two very  different families just try to resist  their meat cute when their love at first  sight is so powerful that they both  begin to levitate  based on the first installment of cs  lewis's beloved children's fantasy  novels 2005's the chronicles of narnia  the lion the witch and the wardrobe  follow the four pevensey children as  they evacuate london during the blitz in  world war ii when they travel to a  countryside mansion containing a  mysterious wardrobe the pevensey clan  are transported to a magical world  overtaken by the white witch played by a  chilly and terrifying tilda swinton and  ultimately join the fight for the fate  of narnia  every stick and stone you see  every icicle  is narnia  james mcavoy plays mr tumnus a  kind-hearted fawn in a red scarf it's a  role that's so memorable that mcavoy  reprised it in 2019 for a saturday night  live skit mcavoy gives tumnas a gentle  charm in his scenes with lucy pevensey  who is played by georgie henley in her  first on-screen role his performance  gives us the first signs of warmth in  this strange world condemned to eternal  winter  split is m night shyamalan's 2016 take  on the super villain origin story  combining hitchcockian suspense with  old-fashioned exploitation movie grit  split is about mcavoy's kevin wendell  crum a man struggling with dissociative  identity disorder and  23 alternate personalities vying for  control of his body this psychological  thriller is a spectacular showcase of  james mcavoy's acting abilities and  would arguably not work without him at  its center mcavoy must play eight  distinct personalities with different  ages speech patterns and mannerisms with  only minimal costume changes and he  rises to the challenge a major point of  concern however is how split portrays  dissociative identity disorder mental  health professionals 